      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-45426",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/ac/ac3f81c643f18d60bdd486c0dad952ed062699b5a086d25a46bd0024ded08282.png)\n\nThe speeds, in $km/s$, of two rockets are given by the equations $y = 0.63x + 6$ and $y = 0.95x + 3$, respectively, where $x$ is the time, in seconds, since they were launched. Using the graph above and the table below, find a value for $x$ that corresponds to an approximation of the point where both rockets have the same speed.\n\n| $x$ | $8.2$ | $9.4$ | $10.0$ | $11.4$ | $11.8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_{1} = 0.63x + 6$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y_{2} = 0.95x + 3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $\\mid y_{1} - y_{2} \\mid$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."
